{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/e2065775ae6372e8c820b5e2f19f6f105e032dcd37db1e32e04af5bfa6a4faf9.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,-2]\\cup (2,4)$\n- $x\\in (0,2]$\n- $x\\in [1,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-36066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,-2]\\cup (2,4)$ and $x\\in (0,2].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/3c5bc724ee29aee7bef2790012bf0e73ce1ed087134213dbad36a670fd136a36.png)\n\n        \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[1,4)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/03b9d70513a4ef99b39c9f05d0c0ac803f8e5611d0967015e9c1d9c0f45bd418.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-36066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33764a26a79503c730a31ff319e01ca2c98f76597f2942669246b7bb08261e60"} {"question" "q-36066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-36066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-36066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/239490fd3be930412d4f67ff89244607a5019d91f201bc4e579410b5610a18ed.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/c728d173c13a1e7b3bcad0e9f0284c97f2ca075c795dcb1f38ed086c19cc0504.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graphs I and IV, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graphs I  and IV show one-to-one functions. This means that  these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II and  III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33764a26a79503c730a31ff319e01ca2c98f76597f2942669246b7bb08261e60"} {"question" "q-66508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and IV only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in (-2,2]$\n- $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Reflect its graph across  the $x$-axis to get $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=-{x}^{2}$  by $1$ unit up to  get $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/83a9f5966c76b89b2cad9c6244e2e447fb0052fc7f946df954df76265628c4a7.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n*The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/f694b6b1bc979289b748758385e05575990bd866457e75a84fbf7bdf335c03b9.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once. Since  $(-2,2]$ is a subset of $(-2,\\infty )$, our function is not invertible on $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$ either.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/c03118f3fd0193910b49e26ab5ea56e4320fd23583feb3267cd4442d0a157583.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33764a26a79503c730a31ff319e01ca2c98f76597f2942669246b7bb08261e60"} {"question" "q-66687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x)=2x+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (10,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in [-5,5)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/d33314230419ec762bc4ad8603b2e6c3436461cd6b4b6fe4783d12a23d01b660.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (10,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-5,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33764a26a79503c730a31ff319e01ca2c98f76597f2942669246b7bb08261e60"} {"question" "q-66724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-66724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-108791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/4fec1c187ae2eaaf40461775425b31d896bdc63bc99233277d6f6bac8949315d.png)\n\nDetermine the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-108791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-3,5].$ \n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in (1,3).$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/eb544795c61be7dd87568e6def10fc907c3554b603c3b344f88ac2a8ff847f8d.png)\n\n- Among the given options, the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ is invertible is $x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5].$ On this domain, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/4059a1ad684023640358af6ee38f0967957336578eb93b3299c9c842e7ce108f.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-108791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "33764a26a79503c730a31ff319e01ca2c98f76597f2942669246b7bb08261e60"} {"question" "q-108791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-108791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in [-3,1)"] ["math" "x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5]"] ["math" "x\\in [-3,5]"] ["math" "x\\in [0,5]"] ["math" "x\\in [1,5]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"} {"question" "q-108791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/state.json" "file_sha256" "70aa159e355836659cd4da261c2b4a2aa9f1327d5513ef97813785d3814ebacd"}] "usage" {} "basis" 872 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
